# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on this machine, so a plain checkout on Windows gives
# every text file CRLF. That is harmless for the files that only ever run
# here — and silently fatal for the ones that get shipped to a Linux host
# and executed there.
#
# `set -euo pipefail\r` makes bash read the option name as "pipefail\r",
# which is not an option, so the script dies on its second line with
# "invalid option name". It cost an evening to find, because ship.sh and
# prepare-host.sh run in Git Bash on Windows, which tolerates the CR — only
# backup.sh, which runs ON the server, ever failed.
#
# Shell scripts and Dockerfiles are LF in the working tree, always,
# whatever anyone's autocrlf says.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by containers or by tooling that does not
# want a stray CR at the end of a value.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
